Project Progress Report: Robust Digital Twin for Aeroelastic Systems
Primary Goal of Research

To develop and validate a robust methodology for real-time state estimation and trajectory forecasting of a nonlinear aeroelastic system using partial and corrupted sensor data, complete with trustworthy uncertainty quantification.

Key Achievements (Summary)

We have designed three distinct digital twin methodologies, culminating in a novel physics-informed neural network architecture for state estimation and forecasting. Furthermore, we have established a comprehensive empirical framework and a set of key performance indicators (KPIs) to rigorously evaluate these models under realistic data corruption scenarios.

Key Achievements (In Detail)

Our progress this period has been focused on establishing a rigorous and comparative research plan, yielding two primary achievements:

1. Design of Three Competing Methodological Approaches:
We have defined a clear A/B/C test for creating the digital twin, allowing us to scientifically evaluate the trade-offs between physical fidelity, data-driven power, and computational feasibility.

Method 1: The Physics-Based Baseline: A standard Unscented Kalman Filter (UKF) that uses the known aeroelastic equations of motion as its predictive engine. This model serves as the benchmark for accuracy and computational speed that more complex models must outperform.

Method 2: The Hybrid SciML Surrogate: This is our primary innovative approach. We designed a novel encoder-decoder neural network architecture to serve as a physics-informed surrogate for the system dynamics.

Architecture: It features three distinct encoders to process the system state, physical parameters (like airspeed), and control inputs. These are fused into a latent space to form a complete "problem specification," which a decoder then uses to forecast the next state.

Advantage: This architecture is designed to be scalable and to generalize across different flight conditions, with the physics-informing constraints intended to improve data efficiency and prediction accuracy.

Method 3: The Purely Data-Driven Model: We analyzed two variants (a sliding-window forward model and a dual-network observer-predictor). This analysis was crucial for understanding the inherent limitations of purely data-driven methods, particularly the problem of long-term forecast instability due to autoregressive error accumulation.

2. Establishment of a Rigorous Empirical Validation Framework:
To move beyond subjective evaluation, we designed a robust "gauntlet" to test and compare the methodologies.

Controlled Stress Test: We defined a data corruption protocol to simulate real-world sensor failures, where trajectories are generated with varying percentages of missing data (e.g., 5-20%) and outlier data (e.g., 5-20% of points with 2-5 STD noise).

Quantitative Key Performance Indicators (KPIs): We defined three critical metrics to build a "performance scorecard" for the models:

Accuracy: Root Mean Squared Forecast Error (RMSFE) to measure raw predictive power.

UQ Honesty: Calibration Error (Coverage Percentage) to measure if the model's self-reported confidence is trustworthy.

UQ Responsiveness: Uncertainty Inflation Ratio to measure how quickly the model recognizes it is "flying blind" during data outages.

Performance Surface Mapping: The final output of this framework will be a set of performance surfaces that map each model's operational limits, providing a clear guide to its reliability under different conditions.

Challenges

Computational Feasibility: The training of the SciML and data-driven models is computationally intensive. A key challenge will be managing the hyperparameter sensitivity study within our available time and computing budget.

Long-Term Stability: Ensuring the long-term stability of any model that includes a data-driven component is non-trivial. Mitigating error accumulation is a primary focus.

Hyperparameter Complexity: The proposed SciML architecture has a large number of hyperparameters (latent dimension, hidden layers, etc.). The planned sensitivity study is essential but will require a methodical and well-documented approach.

Next Quarter Plans

Implementation of Baseline: Implement the Physics-Based Bayesian filter (Method 1) to establish the initial performance benchmark.

Development of SciML Surrogate: Build and train the encoder-decoder neural network (Method 2), including the hyperparameter sensitivity study to find the optimal architecture.

Execute Empirical Study: Run all validated methodologies through the full data corruption grid experiment.

Analyze & Report: Analyze the KPI scorecards and performance surfaces to draw final conclusions on the most robust and reliable methodology.

Transition Planning

The goal is to transition this research into a usable asset for a closed-loop control simulation.

Documentation of Operational Envelope: The final report will feature the "performance surface" plots as a clear guide, advising users on the domain of applicability for the recommended digital twin.

Model Packaging: The code for the winning methodology, including the pre-trained model weights and a functional API, will be packaged for integration.

Integration & Hand-off: The final step will be to support the integration of the digital twin into the main control simulation environment, providing a high-fidelity state estimator for advanced control law development.
